@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "Single entry point and/or middlewares (Reducer)") {
        `Store<R>` — the class the `@Reducer` macro generates for you — is built on the same
        single-entry-point foundation as classic Statostore, so `addMiddleWare` works exactly the
        same way here. Nothing to relearn: you get event interception, logging, and crash
        reporting for free.
    }

    @Section(title: "Logging middleware") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Logging is an important part of the code. Debugging is great, but sometimes the scheduler
            is affected by our breakpoints, and having understandable logs may help us getting rid of
            some bugs.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Let's grab our Reducer counter example, adding some error throwing to illustrate the
                current example.

                @Code(name: "CounterReducer.swift", file: Middleware-Reducer-Counter-01.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                And the CounterView with no protection to avoid the throw.

                @Code(name: "CounterView.swift", file: Middleware-Reducer-CounterView-02.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                `addMiddleWare` can be added to the generated `Store` to trap all events, exactly
                like on a classic Statostore — the closure receives the store, the `When`, and a
                `forward` continuation. Logging is straightforward, either for terminal logging,
                analytics, or crash reports.

                @Code(name: "CounterView.swift", file: Middleware-Reducer-CounterView-03.swift)
            }

            Having a comprehensive crash and analytics reporting is mandatory in production apps.
            A single entry point/middleware enables easy implementation for these features — and
            since it's part of the shared `Statostore`/`ScopeImplementation` foundation, it applies
            identically whether your scope is a hand-written class or a `@Reducer`-generated `Store`.

            This is a different mechanism from `MiddlewareReducer` (documented alongside `@SubState`
            in the Scopes tutorial): `addMiddleWare` sees every event a *single* scope sends to
            itself; `MiddlewareReducer` lets a *parent* Reducer intercept events from its own child
            scopes before they run. Reach for `addMiddleWare` for cross-cutting concerns like logging
            on one scope, and `MiddlewareReducer` when a parent needs to react to what its children do.
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Built-in Library logging") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The error reporting added in the previous step is great. Terminal logging however is
            unnecessary. Statoscope has a built-in feature for logging. See how to use it.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Enable the logging globally in your app.

                @Code(name: "App.swift", file: Middleware-Reducer-Counter-04.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                Rather than eyeballing terminal output, the Reducer pattern's testing-first design
                means you can assert directly on what the middleware observed — `addMiddleWare`
                composes with `GIVEN`/`WHEN`/`THEN` like any other scope behavior.

                @Code(name: "CounterTests.swift", file: Middleware-Reducer-Counter-05.swift)
            }

            The single entry point enables Statoscope to build many of its features internally.
            A middleware can be added to any `Store` — hand-written or macro-generated — so you can
            benefit from the same single entry point regardless of which pattern you're using.
        }
    }
}
